  "final_text": "All four steps are done, using only the Kibana tools.\n\n1. **Data view:** 'Kibana Sample Data Flights' (index `kibana_sample_data_flights`) has time field `timestamp`. Other fields include `Carrier`, `Cancelled`, `Dest`, `Origin`, `FlightDelay`, and `AvgTicketPrice`.\n\n2. **Dashboard:** 'S4-a3326702 Flights' (id `s4-a3326702-flights-edce3a5411a0`) has two panels:\n   - \"Flights over time\": a line chart of count by `timestamp` (date histogram).\n   - \"Flights by carrier\": a bar chart of count by `Carrier` (terms).\n\n3. **Alert rule:** 'S4-a3326702 Cancelled flights' (id `eca4bc9b-e7ed-49ed-b06f-e521a1ce6bf1`) is an `.es-query` rule on `kibana_sample_data_flights` with a 1-minute schedule. It counts documents with `Cancelled: true` over a 5-minute window and fires when the count is greater than 10. It was created disabled, so I enabled it.\n\n4. **Panel edit:** The carrier panel was already titled \"Flights by carrier\" when I built it, so the title didn't change. I re-applied that title with the same spec. The panel's title is correct, but no rename actually happened. Reading the rule back shows `enabled: true` (status `ok`).\n\nChoices I made:\n- The alert query is a term match on `Cancelled: true`, since the rule needs a filter for cancelled flights.\n- The rule's `size` is 100, which is the value from the tool's example.\n\nTIME_FIELD: timestamp\nRULE_ENABLED: yes",
